"""The packs of your own games: music per game, by ear, then the packs, on a terminal screen or
without one (BUILDING.md, "Your own games").

Point it at a folder of ZX Spectrum games (.sna, .z80, .tap, .tzx). Per game, mark one or more
ways to make its music, listen to the results, choose one per score, and build: the studio splits
the games into as many packs as they need, each a full pair of update packages (fmzx.fwsc and
fmzx-preview.fwsc) with each game's chosen music. It needs Python 3.11 or later and nothing
else of its own: run it as tools/pack-studio (through uv) or python3 tools/pack-studio.

  tools/pack-studio <games folder | games file> [-o W] [--sid-dir PATH] [--ay-dir PATH] [--offline]
                    [--cache DIR] [--model NAME]
      Opens the studio (curses). The games are only read; everything goes under the workspace W,
      build/studio/<folder name> unless -o says otherwise (build/ is git-ignored).
      At start, in the background: both device builds (tools/fm1-docker cmake --preset fm1 and
      fm1-preview, then cmake --build with each, in Docker: a no-op when they are up to date;
      FMZX_SKIP_DEVICE_BUILD=1 skips them, and build/fm1 and build/fm1-preview must then exist),
      fmzx-pack and music-preview (cd host && cargo build --release; when music-preview does not
      build, the studio plays the renders tools/arrange-music wrote instead), then the learning
      run:
          fmzx-pack personal <folder> -o W/all --app <fm1 app> --app <fm1-preview app> --plan
      which converts and learns every game and writes W/all/personal-games.toml (the folder's
      games file: a re-run keeps your edits to it), the scores in W/all/arrange/ and
      W/all/plan.json (each game: how it went in, its packed size with its chosen music, its
      music line and its scores). It reports each game on stderr as it goes (fmzx-pack's
      "loaded 3/45: <file> (<how>)", "converted 3/45: <name> (<how>)", "learning 45 games: 27
      from the cache, 18 to learn, 8 at a time", "learning: <name>", "learned 13/45: <name>
      (21.4 s)" or "(cached)"); until the plan is there the games screen lists the games
      (W/all/personal-games.toml's, or the folder's game files before it is written), each
      waiting, converting, learning (for how long), learned (its time, or cached) or left out
      (why), with its converted file's size (~, before packing), under "learning 12 of 45 games
      — about 4 min left" (the games still to learn at the average time of those learned, so
      many at a time). Before a workspace's first learning (no W/all/plan.json), a job copies
      the cache files W/all/sound-cache lacks from the sound-cache/ folders (not links) of other
      personal packs under build/ (at most four folders down, beside a personal-games.toml or a
      converted/manifest.toml, none inside W): fmzx-pack names a cache file
      <crc>-<learner version>.bin, the CRC over the converted game, its key script and its
      overrides, so a copy is the same game's music. A file there is never overwritten; the log
      says "reused 27 learned games from build/zx-tunes" per folder.
      A games file (.toml, the syntax of W/all/personal-games.toml) in place of the folder is
      used as written: the learning reads it (fmzx-pack personal <file> -o W/all ..., a relative
      file from the games file's folder), nothing rewrites it, and the packs take their entries
      from it. Its workspace is the one it lies in when it is a workspace's
      all/personal-games.toml (after you deleted or commented out some entries there), else
      build/studio/<file name without .toml>.
      Long steps run one at a time in the background; their output goes to W/studio.log. The
      bottom line shows the step running, how many wait, and what it is doing (a takes job's
      take and pass, else its last output line; a message of the last 5 s first); J lists every
      job (the jobs view, below).
      --sid-dir PATH   the SID collection to use (HVSC's C64Music, or any folder of .sid files)
                       instead of the one the studio downloads (below)
      --ay-dir PATH    the AY collection to use (a folder holding music/ay, or any folder of
                       .ay files) instead of the one the studio downloads (below)
      --offline        download nothing: SID takes need --sid-dir or a collection already in
                       build/hvsc/, AY takes --ay-dir or one in build/ay/; tools/arrange-music
                       runs with UV_OFFLINE=1 and generate --offline; tools/make-packages with
                       --offline (the SDK files from build/ac79/ only)
      --cache DIR      tools/make-packages --cache DIR: another folder for the SDK files
      --model NAME     the generator for AI takes (tools/arrange-music generate --model: amt, the
                       default, or rules)

  The methods, per game (marked in W/studio.toml):
      F  FM   the FM-1 plays the music learned from the game's code and memory: always there,
              take 0, "the original" (for a shadow score, the routine playing live)
      A  AI   arranged takes (a bass, a pad and a drum around the melody):
              tools/arrange-music generate W/all --game ID [--model NAME], takes take-<n>
      S  SID  the game's C64 tune: tools/arrange-music chip W/all --game ID --sid-dir PATH,
              takes chip-<n>
      Y  AY   the game's Spectrum 128 tune: tools/arrange-music ay W/all --game ID --ay-dir PATH,
              takes ay-<n>
  AI, SID and AY work on a game's scores (recorded or shadow scores, BUILDING.md "Arranged music"); a
  game with none has nothing to arrange: its music plays as FM from its code, or on the beeper.
  tools/arrange-music runs through uv (https://docs.astral.sh/uv/); its first AI job downloads
  its packages and a 1.4 GB model.

  The games screen: a row per game, its name, how it went in, its packed size (with its chosen
  music, as of the last learning), its pack (P1 to P9, a colour per pack, or a dash), its music
  methods ("FM AI SID AY": FM always, the others coloured when marked, dots when not), and per score the choice (original, take-2, chip-1, ay-1), else
  "nothing to arrange: FM from code" or "beeper". Games left out are dimmed, with why. Above the
  rows, the packs: per pack with games "P1  312.4/416.7 KB <bar> 4 games" (its games' bytes
  against what a pack holds: the room less 2% less the pack's overhead, the plan's bytes beyond
  its games, as the split counts), red with how much over when over; then the games in no pack
  ("sizes as of the last learn" when a take was chosen after it). The packs are kept in
  W/studio.toml [packs].
      Up/Down, j/k   move (PgUp/PgDn, Home/End)
      Enter          open the game
      1-9            put the game in that pack; one it would take over its room refuses it (red)
                     until the same key again puts it there anyway (then no build starts)
      0, x           the game in no pack
      f              every packed game in no pack into the first pack with room, in name order
      p              the packs: each pack, its fill and its games (1-9, 0/x move the game chosen,
                     Enter opens it, b builds, Left/h/Esc/p back)
      a / s / y      mark or unmark a way to get new music for the game (nothing runs yet):
                     a AI, the model writes a bass, chords and drums around its melody;
                     s SID, its C64 version's tune from the SID collection;
                     y AY, its Spectrum 128 version's tune from the AY collection
      A / S / Y      the same for every game with scores
      g              generate the marked methods: an AI job per game with A, a SID job per game
                     with S, an AY job per game with Y (the SID or AY collection is downloaded
                     first when there is none); it says how many it queued ("queued 5 jobs (2 AI,
                     3 SID): J shows them"), and a game with a takes job waiting or running has
                     "…" ("*" without UTF-8; yellow while it runs) after its methods
      J              the jobs (below)
      r              learn again (the plan: sizes change with the choices)
      b              build the firmware: the packages (fmzx.fwsc, fmzx-preview.fwsc) of each pack,
                     with each game's chosen music, in W/pack-<n>/
      l              the log (W/studio.log); ? this text; q quit (asks when jobs are running)
  Colours (bold, dim and reverse only on a terminal without them, or with NO_COLOR set): names and
  titles bold, the chosen candidate green, marked methods AI magenta, SID cyan, AY yellow (dim
  when not), errors and refusals red, the bottom line yellow while a step runs, green when one
  is done, red when one failed; while learning, learning yellow, learned green, cached dim green;
  on the jobs view, running yellow, done green, failed red, waiting dim.

  The game screen: the game's scores (key, seconds, shadow or not, what the last plan says
  plays), and per score its candidates:
      beeper                  the game's own sound (to compare; not a choice)
      FM (from code/memory)   take 0
      AI take-<n>             with its key, consonance and notes a second (arranged.toml [metrics])
      SID chip-<n>            with its source and melody match
      C64 original (chip-<n>) the C64's own sound, when tools/arrange-music wrote chip.wav
      AY ay-<n>               with its source and melody match
      Spectrum 128 original (ay-<n>)  the Spectrum 128's own sound, when tools/arrange-music
                              wrote ay.wav
  A game with no scores lists the whole game's beeper and FM, to listen to.
      Up/Down, j/k   move
      Enter, space   play the candidate, or stop it when it is the one playing (playing
                     another stops the one playing)
      Esc            stop (with nothing playing: back)
      c              choose the candidate for its score: written to W/all/arrange/choices.toml
                     at once (as tools/arrange-music pick --set does); the chosen one is marked *
                     and the others not
      a / s / y / g  mark AI / SID / AY for this game, generate them (Y: AY for every game)
      Left, h, Backspace   back; l the log; J the jobs
  What plays: music-preview's render of exactly what the FM-1 plays (W/previews/<game>__<key>__
  <take>.wav, made in the background, "rendering..." until it is ready, then played; kept until
  the take changes); without music-preview, the renders tools/arrange-music wrote: original.wav
  (General MIDI, for take 0), take-<n>/fm1.wav, chip-<n>/fm1.wav, ay-<n>/fm1.wav. A candidate with nothing to
  play says why. The player is afplay on macOS, else ffplay, paplay or aplay; FMZX_PLAYER="cmd
  args" overrides (the file is added last).

  The jobs view (J on every screen; J, Esc, Left or h goes back): every job of the session, run
  one at a time in this order: the one running, those waiting (in the order they run; SID and AY
  takes held until their collection's download is done), then those finished, newest first. Per
  job its state (running m:ss yellow, waiting dim, done in N s green, failed red, cancelled, or
  skipped when a job before it in its chain failed or its collection did not download), what it
  is (AI, SID or AY takes, the method in its colour; a collection's download, the learning, a
  render, a choice, a pack's build), its game, and a line: while an AI job runs, "take 2 of 4,
  pass 'bass', 38% (about 24 min left)" (from tools/arrange-music generate's progress lines: the
  passes drums, bass, chords; the time left from the takes done, else its estimate), a SID or AY
  job "checking candidates: 3 of 12", a download its MB, the learning its header; when it is
  done, its result from tools/arrange-music's summary line (its docstring, "The summary line"):
  "3 takes, best melody match 0.73 (C64: <title> by <author> (<released>))" (with "weak: maybe
  another tune, listen" under 0.15), "no C64 tune found by that name", "0 takes, 4 songs skipped
  (48K beeper or CPC tunes)", "4 takes written, 3 min 12 s", "had its SID takes already:
  nothing to do"; a failed job its exit code and last output line. A finished takes job also
  says its result on the bottom line ("SID takes for <game>: 3 (best match 0.73); Enter on the
  game to listen"). The jobs are the session's (their output is in W/studio.log too).
      Up/Down, j/k   move (PgUp/PgDn, Home/End)
      Enter          the job's own output, scrollable (l: the whole log)
      o              open the job's game, to listen to its takes
      x              cancel: a waiting job leaves the queue at once; the running one, after y, is
                     stopped (SIGTERM to its process group, SIGKILL 5 s later; a download stops at
                     its next block) and marked cancelled

  The build (b, or --build): the plan again (choices change the packed sizes), then the packs
  filled with 1-9 (or f), each as W/pack-<n>/ under its own number (packs with no game skipped);
  a game in no pack is not built (the summary names them), and a pack over what it holds stops
  the build before it starts, naming the pack and by how much. With no game in any pack, the
  packed games in name order fill pack 1 while the total stays within the room minus 2% (and
  the pack's own overhead), then pack 2, and so on. Each pack is
  W/pack-<n>/: personal-games.toml (the header and those games' entries from the games file,
  W/all/personal-games.toml or the one given, as written there, [game.keys] and
  [game.instruments] tables included; a relative file made absolute), the links
  sound-cache -> ../all/sound-cache and arrange -> ../all/arrange (the learned music and the
  choices are shared), and the pack and its packages:
      fmzx-pack personal W/pack-<n>/personal-games.toml -o W/pack-<n> --app <fm1 app> --app <fm1-preview app>
      FMZX_SKIP_DEVICE_BUILD=1 FMZX_PACKAGES_PACK=W/pack-<n>/games.bin FMZX_PACKAGES_OUT=W/pack-<n> \
          tools/make-packages [--offline] [--cache DIR]
  which write the pack's games.bin, keymaps.json, fmzx.fwsc and fmzx-preview.fwsc (and the SDK
  files' licence text beside them), print the pack's table (each game, how it went in, its packed
  size; the room used and free; the music learned) and verify both packages. The build needs
  build/fm1/platform/fm1/fmzx_app.bin, build/fm1-preview/platform/fm1/fmzx_app.bin and
  build/fm1/third_party/felucca-loader/loader.bin (the device builds at start make them).
  A pack of the name-order split that fails with "the pack does not fit" gives its last game to
  the next pack and is built again; a game that does not fit in a pack of its own is left out of
  every pack, and the summary says so (a pack you filled that does not fit fails the build).
  The other packs the studio made (a W/pack-<m>/.pack-studio marker) are removed. The summary lists each pack, its games and its fmzx.fwsc; the studio prints
  it again when it closes. Install a pack's fmzx.fwsc as docs/install.md describes.

  The SID collection: with no --sid-dir, the studio uses build/hvsc/<version>/ (repo root),
  downloading it on the first SID job: the High Voltage SID Collection, version and address from
  https://hvsc.c64.org/api/v1/version/7z (about 85 MB, progress on the bottom line), streamed to
  a .part file and renamed when complete, then unpacked into build/hvsc/<version>/ (about 380 MB,
  half a minute), the archive then deleted. bsdtar (libarchive: macOS's tar), else 7-Zip (7zz, 7z
  or 7za), lists the members first (tar -tf and tar -tvf, or 7z l -slt): a member whose path is
  absolute or holds "..", or that is a link or a device, refuses the whole archive, as does more
  than 8 GB unpacked; the same tool then unpacks it. Every path unpacked is checked to stay
  inside, any link is removed, and nothing in the archive is ever run. The collection is the
  folder holding DOCUMENTS/STIL.txt (C64Music). The tunes belong to their authors: keep the
  collection and the packs made with it to yourself (BUILDING.md, "Music from other versions").

  The AY collection: with no --ay-dir, the studio uses build/ay/<commit>/music/ay (repo root),
  downloading it on the first AY job: the chris-y/AY repository on GitHub (Spectrum AY music;
  music/ay/games/ holds the games' tunes, music/ay/misc/ more), its newest commit from
  https://api.github.com/repos/chris-y/AY/commits/main, its archive from
  https://codeload.github.com/chris-y/AY/tar.gz/<commit> (about 30 MB, progress on the bottom
  line), streamed to a .part file and renamed when complete, then read as a stream: only the
  members under music/ay are unpacked, into build/ay/<first 12 digits of the commit>/; a member
  whose path is absolute or holds "..", or one under music/ay that is a link or a device,
  refuses the whole archive; every path unpacked is checked to stay inside, the archive is then
  deleted, and nothing in it is ever run. The same holds for these tunes: keep them and the
  packs made with them to yourself.

  Without the screen: any of --assign, --auto-fill, --generate, --build, --status and --try
  (several run in that order; a step that fails stops the command, exit 1). They take the
  options above, and print the learning's progress lines as they come.
  tools/pack-studio <games> --assign GAME=N [--assign GAME=N]... [--auto-fill]
      Puts each game given (its id) in pack N (1 to 9; 0: in none), refused when that takes the
      pack over what it holds; --auto-fill then does what f does. Prints the packs. Learns first
      when W/all/plan.json is missing.
  tools/pack-studio <games> --generate ai,sid,ay [--game ID]...
      Marks the methods named (one or more of ai, sid, ay, comma-separated) for every game with
      scores, or for each game given with --game (its id, as --status shows it), and runs the
      jobs g runs for them, one at a time (the SID or AY collection downloaded first when there
      is none); learns first when W/all/plan.json is missing. Choosing a take stays a choice by
      ear: the screen, or tools/arrange-music pick W/all.
  tools/pack-studio <games> --build
      The start steps and the build, without the screen; prints the summary. Exit 1 when a step
      fails or a game fits in no pack.
  tools/pack-studio <games> --status
      Prints the games, their methods, packs, scores, candidates and choices, the packs' fill,
      the packs a build would make and the packs built; runs the start steps and the learning first when W/all/plan.json
      is missing.
  tools/pack-studio <games> --try [N]
      The browser test bench on pack N's packages (tools/web-demo --packages W/pack-<N>; N is 1
      unless given), after the build when --build is given too, else on the packs built before.
  tools/pack-studio selftest
      Checks the progress lines and the list while learning (states, the switch to the games
      file, the estimate), the reuse of earlier learning (only missing files copied, never over
      one, the workspace and links not read), the colours (NO_COLOR), the packs filled (kept in
      studio.toml, refused over the room, f, --assign and --auto-fill, the build of exactly
      those, one over its room refused), the split, the games-file subsets ([game.keys] tables kept), the pack folders and
      their links, each pack's fmzx-pack and tools/make-packages runs, the stale-pack removal, the
      retry when a pack does not fit, a games file as the input, the methods file, generate and
      choose through --set (AI, SID and AY, on the screen's path and through --generate), --try,
      the previews, --status, the jobs (their states, a waiting one cancelled, a running one
      stopped, takes held for a download that fails, the result of AI, SID and AY jobs from
      tools/arrange-music's output: a C64 tune found, none found by name, songs the converter
      refuses, the progress of an AI job; what g says and the games marked), the SID and AY downloads (from a server on 127.0.0.1) and the safe
      unpacking (archive listings with a path escaping their folder, a link or a device; a
      made-up .7z, written and read by bsdtar when it is there, and .tar.gz, and ones with a
      path escaping their folder or a link), and the screen (in a pseudo-terminal, resized down
      to 1x1; the jobs view and a job's output at 80x24; while learning, in colour and with
      NO_COLOR), in a temporary folder with stand-ins for every tool: no network, no Docker, no
      cargo.

Stand-ins (the selftest's; the real tools are the default): FMZX_PACK (the fmzx-pack binary;
no cargo build of it then), FMZX_ARRANGE_MUSIC, FMZX_MAKE_PACKAGES, FMZX_WEB_DEMO,
FMZX_MUSIC_PREVIEW (no cargo build of it then), FMZX_BUILD (the folder holding the device builds
fm1/ and fm1-preview/, instead of build/), FMZX_HVSC_API (the version query's address),
FMZX_HVSC_DIR (instead of build/hvsc), FMZX_AY_API (the commit query's address),
FMZX_AY_TARBALL (the archive's address, {sha} the commit), FMZX_AY_DIR (instead of build/ay),
FMZX_REUSE_FROM (the folder searched for earlier learning instead of build/; empty: none).
"""

